most high school seniors probably don't  spend a lot of time watching the us  treasury market but thanks to this  year's sell-off it will be a lot more  expensive for them to get a loan for  college student loans distributed this  year will carry the highest rate in  years  4.99 percent i'm going to explain how  federal student loan interest rates are  calculated why rates will be so much  higher this year after touching a record  low just two years ago and what this  means for the cost of a college  education let's get into it the  department of education determined  student loan interest rates by combining  a fixed add-on rate with the yield on  10-year us government bonds at the last  auction in may  that just happened undergraduate  students receiving loans from the  federal government get the lowest rate  for graduate students it'll be a little  more than one and a half percentage  points higher and for professionals or  those receiving a parent plus loan the  rate will be a little more than two and  a half percentage points higher one of  the few good things to come from the  coronavirus pandemic was that students  who started college in 2020 got to lock  in the lowest student loan rates in  history the pandemic brought on a  recession and expectations for weak  future economic growth which kept  treasury yields near record lows in may  unfortunately for students headed to  college this fall those days are over  treasury yields in 2022 have risen at  the fastest pace on record to start a  year that has been the result of high  inflation and expectations for the  federal reserve to raise u.s interest  rates at an accelerated clip because the  10-year yield spiked leading up to may's  final auction the rate locked in by  student loan borrowers will be the  highest in years unlike with other loans  the rate on student loans is not  influenced by credit score or financial  profile this will make the cost of  borrowing for college a lot more  expensive  undergraduate borrowers who got their  student loan in 2020 and borrowed the  maximum 5 500 in direct loans will pay  about eight hundred dollars in interest  over ten years that same borrower who  got a loan last year will pay almost  eleven hundred dollars in interest and  this year that borrower will pay almost  fifteen hundred dollars in interest  that's almost double what they paid two  years ago  and while the interest rate doesn't  change during the year students  borrowing for the typical four years of  college will get a different loan rate  each year the rate for federal student  loans is generally lower than private  student loans but not always since those  are based on credit and typically  require a cosigner for the student  there's currently about 1.75 trillion  dollars of student loan debt outstanding  that's likely to rise even more soon  unless president biden goes through with  his controversial plan to forgive them  and with higher interest rates driving  up the price tag for college expect this  to become an even more hot button issue  paul wahl the people's champ taught us  how to deal with times like these he  said chasing checks now place your bets  i bet on me  now it's time to collect  numbers going up  price is going up  i'll see you next time
 

